// expect-error: points into a PACKED struct field
// BUG-972: THIS REJECTION IS AN OVER-REJECTION, pinned deliberately.
//
// `&p.w[0]` where `w` is a BYTE array cannot be misaligned — u8 has alignment 1. The
// file is here, as a negative, because that is what the compiler DOES today and the
// behaviour should not drift silently; it is not here because the rejection is right.
//
// Why it is accepted as a cost: peeling INDEX in addr_of_is_packed_field closed the
// real hole (`&p.w[0]` on a `u32[2]` field — offsetof 1, a misaligned 32-bit store,
// tests/zer_fail/packed_array_elem_addr.zer) and made the byte case rejected as a side
// effect. That is UNIFORM with pre-existing behaviour: `&p.b` on a plain `u8` field of
// a packed struct was already rejected. So it adds no new inconsistency, and the
// measured corpus cost is zero — the suite is green across the 18 files using packed
// structs.
//
// Note the deliberate ASYMMETRY with the SLICE half of the same fix, which IS
// alignment-aware (`packed_array_field_view` keys on type_alignment_bytes, so
// `[*]u8 v = f.payload;` compiles — tests/zer/packed_u8_array_view_ok.zer). The slice
// rule is new, so its precision was designed in; the address-of rule is old, and
// making it alignment-aware would RELAX a shipped rule — the accept-unsafe change
// class, which belongs in its own commit with its own negative matrix.
//
// FIX SKETCH: gate addr_of_is_packed_field on type_alignment_bytes(target) > 1, which
// also relaxes `&p.b`. Do it as a standalone relaxation per the "sound relaxation"
// discipline in docs/compiler-internals.md. When that lands, DELETE this file.

packed struct P { u8 a; u8[4] w; }
u32 main() {
    P p;
    *u8 q = &p.w[0];
    *q = 1;
    return (u32)p.w[0] - 1;
}
